# 7. AUDIO ENGINE — NONNEGOTIABLE

Build a real-time-capable audio subsystem.

The application cannot be considered successful if it only creates MIDI objects without producing audible sound.

### Architecture

Define an AudioEngine interface abstracting:

- Initialization.
- Start.
- Stop.
- Pause.
- Sample rate.
- Buffer configuration.
- Note on.
- Note off.
- Sample triggering.
- Track gain.
- Track pan.
- Voice allocation.
- Audio rendering.
- Effects processing.
- Offline rendering.
- Device routing and lifecycle.

Use an Android-compatible implementation for this initial build.

Prefer a continuously running audio rendering architecture over creating individual Android media-player instances for every note.

Use AudioTrack or another justified Android audio approach that supports multiple synthesized/sample voices mixed into one output stream.

Evaluate Android's low-latency audio capabilities and request appropriate usage/performance settings where available.

Use AudioManager for audio-device and focus management.

Do not claim guaranteed low latency across all Android devices.

### Timing

Maintain one master musical clock.

Use high-resolution monotonic timing.

Do not use Compose animations, UI frame timing, or Handler delays as the authoritative musical clock.

Schedule events ahead of playback.

Convert musical ticks into sample-frame positions.

Maintain precise loop boundaries.

Support accurate note-on and note-off scheduling.

Avoid drift across repeated loops.

Define a clear transport state machine.

### Timebase

Use integer musical ticks with a documented PPQ resolution, preferably 960 ticks per quarter note.

Use sample-frame positions for actual rendering.

Define conversions between:

- BPM.
- Beats.
- Bars.
- Ticks.
- Seconds.
- Sample frames.

Ensure floating-point rounding does not accumulate across long playback sessions.

### Voice engine

Support polyphonic playback.

Implement voice allocation and release.

Add configurable or sensible polyphony limits.

Avoid stuck notes.

Implement an all-notes-off command.

Handle retriggering and overlapping notes.

### Mixing

Mix active voices into each track.

Apply track gain and pan.

Sum tracks into the master bus.

Provide headroom.

Apply a simple master peak limiter to prevent severe digital clipping.

Avoid hard clipping and audible distortion under normal use.

Provide meter values derived from actual audio, not fabricated animation.

### Stability

Audio rendering must not depend on UI recomposition.

Do not allocate large objects inside the audio-rendering hot path.

Do not perform file I/O inside time-critical rendering loops.

Do not make network calls in the audio subsystem.

Do not block audio processing while updating the library, editing notes, or switching views.

Avoid clicks when transport stops, notes end, or parameters change.

Use fades or smoothing where appropriate.

Handle audio focus loss, interruptions, sample-rate changes, and device disconnection.

Keep sound functioning with the screen locked when the user explicitly enables background playback.

Use an appropriate foreground service and media notification where Android requires it.

Do not consume microphone input unless recording is explicitly requested.

